From 71bc61a77e50bb27a502df3c8e0e2bb56bba114a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:02:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): hold queued archive preflight until reconnect
 [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A transport outage is not an expiry of the user's archive intent. Retain the
preflight queue without an overall reconnect deadline, while keeping each full
safety read independently bounded. Keep the owner/profile pinned. Re-read the
whole ancestry and task status after reconnect; unknown is never automatic
permission. Leave the generic bounded session-write helper's default unchanged.

Show the standing "Will archive when reconnected" status with a Keep chat
cancellation action. Cancellation interrupts both backoff and in-flight reads,
restores the optimistic row through the existing action boundary, and surfaces a
visible cancellation error. Late results cannot advance the cancelled action.

Use the explicitly allowed reload-drop branch of the requirement: localStorage
records the queued intent's existence per window, not a safety verdict or PATCH.
A new renderer reports a persistent dropped-archive warning on reload/restart,
then removes the marker. Active remounts do not clear their queue. Migrate old
sessionStorage markers too. No automatic mutation is replayed after a reload.

## Five whys

Problem: a laptop archive attempt for a chat
queued on a 14s preflight timeout,
then failed about 60s in. A second manual attempt later
succeeded. Source evidence and original STATE.md were read from the attachment
on predecessor t_023f1c8dd582e773 (PR #4013).

1. Why was another click necessary? The original queued preflight rejected its
   transport failure instead of keeping the intent alive. Evidence: desktop.log
   deadline error after about 60 seconds and later second start; reproduced on main by the
   new six-minute-outage test.
2. Why did it reject at about 60 seconds? reconnectArchivePreflight reused
   withSessionWriteReconnect's default SESSION_WRITE_RECONNECT_BUDGET_MS=60000.
3. Why does that budget not cover the observed incident? The Mac's connection
   continued timing out for minutes, including 4000/10000ms hub requests at
   timeouts around the successful manual archive. Hub service to other clients
   did not imply that this Desktop route could complete readiness and reads.
4. Why did the prior fix conflate those lifetimes? It correctly bounded each
   unknown safety read, but put the reconnect wait in the same finite retry
   abstraction used for ordinary session writes. Safety evidence expires;
   waiting user intent must not. This patch separates them without converting
   transport-unknown into an Archive anyway confirmation.
5. Why was the failure not caught? Tests in archive-reconnect,
   confirm-linked-archive, archive-deadline and the real useSessionActions
   harness explicitly expected deadline failure/restoration, not recovery after
   a multi-minute outage. New coverage demands no second action after >5min,
   one trace UUID through committed, persisted reload-drop visibility, and
   cancellation during backoff/in-flight work.

Root (happened): a finite overall transport wait was used as the lifetime of a
queued archive preflight. Root (not caught): exhaustion was an asserted success
condition; no beyond-five-minute recovery or app-restart durable marker test.
Recurrence: follows #4013; that PR repaired fail-fast preflight transport loss,
but its default 60s window was still shorter than real connection failures.

### Separate transport cause: explicitly UNDETERMINED

Owner: t_d73f672e4582f2f7, "Desktop laptop→hub transport: determine why requests
time out for minutes while other clients succeed". No transport fix here.
Its read-only investigation reports a long-lived direct SSH master and retained
forward 65345; natural route probes sometimes chose a functioning jump path
while direct was incomplete. The helper selects once for that SSH master;
later route probes cannot move an existing master. This narrows, but does NOT
prove, the incident cause. Hub WS accepts and zero-message client closes were
observed, so do not claim requests never reached the hub. Local Agent queuing,
SSH/link stalls and hub readiness/endpoint latency remain inseparable without
per-stage/request-ID timing. Normal request timeouts do not mark an up
remote-down gate down (electron/remote-down-gate.ts); that code fact alone is
not a root-cause proof. Historical and live timeout clearance are not claimed.

### Bundle/backend alignment and update handoff

Read-only remote inspection of the laptop: initially the checkout HEAD and installed
Resources/install-stamp.json both named
2f204cb41aaf399971e2fdb80dd094c8d1745dc1; the stamp builtAt was
an earlier build timestamp. A subsequent investigation read observed checkout HEAD
4aa9d2a8876cb4b0125b20582683d2d958e06bb2 while the bundle still named 2f204cb4.
Checkout HEAD is NOT proof of the currently loaded backend's build identity.
The original 409 client_upgrade_required cause stays UNDETERMINED:
historical evidence is no longer available. Today's observations do not close
that historical causal question.

The updater is not an atomic bundle/backend pair: hermes-sync's apply path sets
HERMES_UPDATE_DEFER_DESKTOP_SWAP when Desktop is open; source_build.py's
_build_desktop_product deliberately leaves the bundle in place. A later
maybe_swap_desktop/swap_desktop_bundle installs a prepared app after quit or an
allowed idle window. The desktop_update posix handoff similarly follows the
committed code update with a separate transactional bundle swap. core-wt deliver
expedites that existing path; it is not a combined atomic commit. Existing
contract-skew/lag mitigations must be tested, not presumed absent.
Separate regression owner t_9d48be796bbb7387 covers these paths and existing
mitigations, including a failed bundle swap after code advancement. No updater
or transport code is changed in this archive PR.

## Verification

RED on unchanged main: 4 of the original 5 new durable-queue tests fail for the
expected lifetime, cancellation and restart-marker assertions; nontransport
unknown control passes. GREEN covers six-minute outage then automatic commit,
full ancestry/tasks reread, owner pinning, cancellation and ignored late reads,
nontransport unknown confirmation, new-renderer durable drop notice, and the
real archiveSession hook with a >5min outage. Renderer typecheck passes using
an isolated deps worktree. Required exact-head CI and independent review remain
mandatory before canonical hermes-core-wt ship landing.

## Live gate (not yet proven)

After installation on laptop, passively read desktop.log and rotations only.
Require one natural stale-window attempt start -> queued -> committed under one
UUID, no second start for that chat in 10min, and hub archived=1 readback.
No Mac app/backend/tunnel launch, fault injection, or custom proof coordinator.
Park with a log-grep wake check and seven-day backstop; if no natural overlap
occurs, record UNPROVEN rather than fabricate proof.

